.TH "HDW4S\-SHARED\-SWEEP" "8" "October 2026" "hdw4s" "hdw4s"
.SH "NAME"
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \- guard, sweep and relay for the hdw4s /shared drop area
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fItabledir\fR \fB\-\-idle\fR \fIduration\fR \fB\-\-max\-age\fR \fIduration\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-watch\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fItabledir\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-guard\fR \fIdirectory\fR [\fB\-\-form\fR \fBany\fR|\fBi\fR|\fBii\fR]
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-harden\fR \fIstore\fR
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-check\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fIdirectory\fR \fB\-\-idle\fR \fIduration\fR [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIduration\fR] [\fB\-\-verbose\fR]
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-relay\-server\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fItabledir\fR [\fB\-\-group\fR \fIgroup\fR] [\fB\-\-interface\fR \fInames\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\fR \fIhost\fR[,\fIhost\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]] [\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIaddr\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIport\fR] [\fB\-\-silence\fR \fIduration\fR] [\fB\-\-heartbeat\fR \fIduration\fR]
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-relay\-client\fR \fB\-\-store\fR \fIstore\fR \fB\-\-idle\fR \fIduration\fR [\fB\-\-group\fR \fIgroup\fR] [\fB\-\-ttl\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-interface\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-to\fR \fIaddr\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR \fIport\fR] [\fB\-\-heartbeat\fR \fIduration\fR]
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-probe\-sandbox\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fItabledir\fR [\fB\-\-probe\-path\fR \fBr\fR|\fBw\fR:\fIpath\fR]\|\.\|\.\|\.
.br
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fB/shared\fR is a drop area that every hdw4s desktop on a machine, and the machine's administrator, can read and write: anybody may leave, take, replace or change anything there, and nothing about an item is promised\. This tool keeps it from becoming a route to anything else, and keeps it tidy\.
.P
It does three jobs, each from its own unit:
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The \fBsweep\fR (\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep@\.timer\fR, every two minutes) removes anything that is not a regular file or a directory, makes every file readable and writable by everybody (\fB(mode & 0777) | 0666\fR, which also clears set\-id bits) and every directory \fB0777\fR, and removes what nobody has used for the idle time or what is older than the maximum age\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The \fBwatcher\fR (\fBhdw4s\-shared\-watch@\.service\fR) does the first two at once, when an item appears, wherever the kernel allows it\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The optional \fBrelay\fR carries "this file was read" from an NFS client to the store's server, where the sweep runs; see \fITHE RELAY\fR\.
.IP "" 0
.P
On a machine running hdw4s, hdw4s starts the sweep and the watcher for the store it owns; see hdw4s(8)\. This package exists on its own for the machine that owns a store shared with others, such as a storage node or a hypervisor that binds the store into containers\.
.SH "THE STORE RULE"
A \fBstore\fR is a whole filesystem dedicated to \fB/shared\fR\. Its root is owned by root and is not group\- or other\-writable, and it holds one directory, \fBtable\fR (mode \fB0777\fR, not sticky), and nothing else except \fBlost+found\fR and \fB\.zfs\fR\. Only \fBtable\fR is ever exposed to desktops or swept\.
.P
Every view of it must be mounted \fBnosymfollow\fR, \fBnodev\fR, \fBnoexec\fR and \fBnosuid\fR, and, unless the filesystem is NFS, \fBstrictatime\fR\. These flags are what make it safe for root to walk a tree anybody can change: no path through it follows a symbolic link, nothing in it runs or acts as a device by being opened, and a separate filesystem means nothing in it is a hard link to a file anywhere else\. On NFS, access times are the server's: mount the store there with access times recorded on every read (on ZFS: \fBatime=on relatime=off\fR)\. This tool cannot verify that from a client\.
.SH "THE GUARD"
Every mode starts by judging the directory it was given, and refuses to touch anything if the judgement fails\. A directory is accepted in exactly one of two forms:
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fB(i)\fR it is the root of a bind of a store's table, which is how desktops see it\. Two things prove it, and both are required: another mount of the same filesystem, visible on the same machine, is a store that passes form (ii), and this mount's root is that store's \fBtable\fR itself (the same device and inode); AND, on a local filesystem, the mount's root within its filesystem is \fB/table\fR, while over NFS \-\- where a bind of a subdirectory records its root as \fB/\fR \-\- the mount's source is the store's source followed by \fB/table\fR\. Anything else, a \fBtable\fR directory of another filesystem included, is refused (\fBno\-sibling\fR);
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fB(ii)\fR it is the directory \fBtable\fR directly beneath the root of a mount whose root is \fB/\fR, and that mount satisfies the store rule\.
.IP "" 0
.P
The sweep, the watcher and the relay server change things, and accept form (ii) only: they are always given a store's own table\. Form (i) is for \fB\-\-check\fR and \fB\-\-guard\fR, which only look\.
.P
The mount is found from the directory's own mount id (statx), never by matching paths, and must carry the flags above\. \fB\-\-guard\fR prints one line and exits 0 when it accepts, and prints the clause that failed and exits 1 when it refuses: \fBopen\fR, \fBpath\fR (the path resolves somewhere else, through a symbolic link), \fBmount\fR, \fBflags\fR, \fBform\-i\fR, \fBstacked\fR, \fBnot\-mount\-child\fR, \fBnot\-table\fR, \fBno\-sibling\fR, \fBfs\-root\fR, \fBstore\-owner\fR, \fBstore\-extra\fR, \fBmoved\fR (a running watcher or relay finds the store unmounted or remounted under it) or, for \fB\-\-harden\fR, \fBremount\fR\.
.P
Independently of the guard, every removal and every change of mode is made relative to an open directory, on a single name, and only after checking, immediately before the call, that the directory is still on the table's mount and still beneath the table, that the entry is on the same mount and is the one that was judged, and, for a change of mode, that the file has no other link\.
.P
Inside an unprivileged container the store root, owned by the host's root, appears owned by the overflow uid (65534); that is accepted there, and only there\.
.P
A stat on an NFS store whose server is down blocks; run the guard under timeout(1) where that matters\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-dir\fR \fItabledir\fR
The directory to sweep, watch, serve or check\. A store's \fBtable\fR, or a bind of it\.
.TP
\fB\-\-idle\fR \fIduration\fR
Remove an item once none of its access, modification and change times is newer than this (\fB30m\fR by default in the units)\. A directory is judged by its modification and change times only, because listing it moves its access time, and only once it is empty\. A time more than five minutes in the future is ignored\.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-age\fR \fIduration\fR
Remove an item this long after it was created, however much it is used (\fB7d\fR by default)\. Needs the filesystem to report a birth time; where it does not, the maximum age is not enforced, and the sweep and \fB\-\-check\fR say so\.
.TP
\fB\-\-harden\fR \fIstore\fR
Check the store rule, then add any missing flag to \fIstore\fR by remounting it in place, then guard its \fBtable\fR\. A path that is not a store is refused before anything is remounted\.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Print what \fBhdw4s check\fR reports about /shared, one \fBred:\fR or \fBwarn:\fR line each: the guard refusing; nobody sweeping (an item unused for longer than the idle time plus two intervals is still there); over 90% full; an NFS store mounted \fBhard\fR; no birth time\. Reads only\.
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIduration\fR
The sweep's period, for \fB\-\-check\fR (default \fB2m\fR)\.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-depth\fR \fIlevels\fR
How deep below the table the sweep, the watcher and \fB\-\-check\fR go (default 8192, and never more than the open files the process may hold)\. A directory deeper than that is not entered, so nothing below it is widened or expired; the sweep counts them and \fB\-\-check\fR warns while there are any\.
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR[\fB=1\fR|\fB=0\fR]
For the sweep and the watcher: every removal and every widening that passes the checks is NOT made, and is logged instead as one line, \fBwould expire\fR, \fBwould remove\-non\-file\fR, \fBwould rmdir\fR or \fBwould widen\fR, with the item's name relative to the table \-\- the only time this tool logs a name\. The pass summary says what would have been done\. A nest of empty directories shows only its deepest level, since nothing beneath is really removed\. In the units, \fBHDW4S_SHARED_DRYRUN=1\fR in a drop\-in turns it on\.
.TP
\fB\-\-unsandboxed\fR
The sweep and the watcher refuse to run as root on the host with a writable root filesystem, which is what running them outside their units' \fBProtectSystem=strict\fR looks like\. This says it is meant\.
.TP
\fB\-\-probe\-sandbox\fR
Try to open a few files outside the table (write: \fB/proc/sys\fR, \fB/sys\fR; read: \fB/etc/shadow\fR, and each \fB\-\-probe\-path\fR), and to touch \fItabledir\fR\fB/canary\fR; print only whether each open succeeded\. Run it in place of a unit's command, with a drop\-in that replaces \fBExecStart=\fR, to see what that unit's sandbox actually allows\.
.P
Durations are a number with one of systemd's unit letters, \fBs\fR \fBm\fR \fBh\fR \fBd\fR \fBw\fR \fBM\fR \fBy\fR (\fBm\fR is minutes, \fBM\fR months), or a bare number of days\. The shortest is \fB2m\fR, and \fB0\fR is refused: expiry is not optional\.
.SH "UNITS"
Every unit is a template whose instance is the escaped path of the store's \fBroot\fR: \fBsystemd\-escape \-\-path /srv/shared\-store\fR gives \fBsrv\-shared\ex2dstore\fR\. Each is bound to that path's mount unit, so it stops when the store is unmounted and is never left holding it busy\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-harden@\.service\fR: on the machine that mounts the store from its device, enable it so that every mount is given its flags before it is exported or bound anywhere\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep@\.timer\fR, \fBhdw4s\-shared\-watch@\.service\fR: enable them on exactly one machine per store, the one that owns its storage\. Change the idle time and maximum age with a drop\-in setting \fBHDW4S_SHARED_IDLE=\fR and \fBHDW4S_SHARED_MAX_AGE=\fR in the \fB\.service\fR\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBhdw4s\-shared\-relay\-server@\.service\fR, \fBhdw4s\-shared\-relay\-client@\.service\fR: off unless enabled; see below\.
.IP "" 0
.P
The sweep and the watcher run as root with only \fBCAP_FOWNER\fR, \fBCAP_DAC_OVERRIDE\fR and \fBCAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH\fR, under \fBProtectSystem=strict\fR plus \fBReadOnlyPaths=/run\fR (strict alone leaves \fB/run\fR writable), with only the store writable\. The watcher also has \fBCAP_SYS_ADMIN\fR for its one fanotify mark and drops it immediately after\. \fBProtectHome=\fR covers \fB/home\fR, \fB/root\fR and \fB/run/user\fR only: a machine whose home directories are elsewhere should add \fBInaccessiblePaths=\fR for them in a drop\-in\.
.P
The sandbox is fixed when a unit starts\. A filesystem the host mounts later \-\- a homes dataset brought up at runtime, an NFS remount \-\- appears inside an already running watcher or relay server writable, whatever \fBProtectSystem=\fR says\. \fBInaccessiblePaths=\fR on its PARENT directory does cover mounts made under it later, so name the parent of any homes tree that may be mounted at runtime, not the tree itself\. The watcher and the relay server are restarted once a day (\fBRuntimeMaxSec=1d\fR) so that their view is renewed; the sweep starts afresh at every run\.
.P
For example, on a storage node whose store is mounted at \fB/srv/shared\-store\fR:
.IP "" 4
.nf
systemctl enable \-\-now 'hdw4s\-shared\-harden@srv\-shared\ex2dstore\.service'
systemctl enable \-\-now 'hdw4s\-shared\-sweep@srv\-shared\ex2dstore\.timer'
systemctl enable \-\-now 'hdw4s\-shared\-watch@srv\-shared\ex2dstore\.service'
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "SHARING ONE STORE BETWEEN MACHINES"
Mount the store on the \fBhost\fR, with the flags, and bind it into each container, carrying the flags on the bind\. If the store is remote, the host mounts it over NFS (\fBsoft\fR, a short \fBtimeo\fR, and the four flags)\. Run the sweep and the watcher only on the machine that owns the storage: access times are truthful there, and fanotify is refused on NFS and on a host's filesystem seen from inside a container\. On every desktop machine set \fBHDW4S_SHARED=source\fR and \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SWEEP=external\fR; see hdw4s(8)\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBLXC, Proxmox VE:\fR in the container's configuration, \fBlxc\.mount\.entry: /srv/shared\-store srv/shared none bind,create=dir,optional,nosymfollow,nodev,noexec,nosuid,strictatime 0 0\fR\. \fBoptional\fR lets the container start when the store is missing; the guard then refuses the empty directory\. Proxmox's \fBmpN:\fR also works, but takes part in snapshots, backups and migration\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBIncus, LXD:\fR a disk device from the host's flagged mount\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBA virtual machine:\fR an NFS mount carrying the flags\.
.IP "" 0
.P
For high availability, present the store at one path on every node (locally where it lives, over NFS elsewhere), so the container's entry is valid wherever it runs\.
.P
On ZFS, the mount helper refuses \fBnosymfollow\fR: mount the dataset plainly (\fBmountpoint=legacy\fR, from fstab) and let \fBhdw4s\-shared\-harden@\fR add the flags\. Exclude the dataset from automatic snapshots and replication, and set \fBquota\fR as well as \fBrefquota\fR: snapshots of a busy drop area are not bounded by \fBrefquota\fR\.
.SH "THE RELAY"
Reads served from an NFS client's page cache never reach the server, so an item read only through an NFS client can expire up to the idle time after it was last written or first read there\. The relay closes that gap\. It is optional and off by default; without it, nothing else changes\. Once enabled it needs no configuration\.
.P
It is meant for HOSTS: the server on the machine that owns the store, the client on each machine that mounts it over NFS, neither in a container\. A tmpfs store, or any store whose sweep runs on the machine its readers use, never needs it\.
.P
\fBMulticast, by default\.\fR The client sends to the group 239\.255\.47\.48 on UDP port 4748 (IPv4 local scope), out of the interface of its route to the store's NFS server, so it follows the store wherever it moves\. The server joins that group on every interface that is up, multicast\-capable, not loopback and has an IPv4 address, and logs which at start\. Whichever nodes are up, on one network segment, this just works\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBTTL 1: one segment\.\fR Nodes on different subnets: raise the TTL (\fB\-\-ttl\fR, \fBHDW4S_SHARED_RELAY_TTL=\fR) where multicast is routed, or configure unicast\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBIGMP snooping\.\fR A switch that snoops IGMP but has no querier forgets a group after a few minutes\. The server re\-issues its membership every minute (an unsolicited report), which keeps such a switch forwarding\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBFirewall\.\fR On the storage host, allow UDP to 239\.255\.47\.48 port 4748 from the client hosts on the interface that faces them\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBWhat is on the wire, and who can read it\.\fR Announcements carry the table\-relative name and fileid of each file opened through an NFS client, as it is used, so they also say WHEN it was used\. They are not encrypted, and any host on the segment can read them: it need only join the group, and with TTL 1 and no IGMP querier they flood the segment anyway, including any guest network bridged onto it\. Nothing else is sent \-\- never contents, and never which user opened a file, only which client host's announcement it was\. Under the default threat model this is harmless: the names are already visible in every desktop's \fB/shared\fR, to everyone who can use it\. If it is not acceptable that other hosts on the segment can see which files are used and when, configure unicast instead (below); the cost is upkeep, because then the server's address and the list of client hosts are kept by hand\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBThe port\.\fR The server does not set \fBSO_REUSEPORT\fR\. A local process on the storage host could still bind the port first and receive or squat the announcements; only an administrator there can\.
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBUnicast, if configured:\fR the client's \fB\-\-to\fR (\fBHDW4S_SHARED_RELAY_TO=\fR) names the server; the server's \fB\-\-listen\fR (\fBHDW4S_SHARED_RELAY_LISTEN=\fR) names the one address it binds \-\- never a wildcard \-\- and \fB\-\-allow\fR is then required\. Announcements then go only to that address\. Nothing is discovered: every client host must be added to \fB\-\-allow\fR, and every client's \fB\-\-to\fR changed whenever the server's address does (a store that moves between hosts takes its server with it), or that client's reads silently stop keeping anything alive until somebody notices the server's warning about its silence\.
.P
The \fBclient\fR watches every directory of the table with inotify and sends "this file was used" for each open it sees, at most once per file per quarter of the idle time, plus a heartbeat every ten minutes (\fB\-\-heartbeat\fR)\. It never reads file contents and never listens\.
.P
It does not see an open in a directory it is not yet watching: one created on its own host an instant before the open (the open can race the new watch), or one created anywhere else \-\- on the storage host or through another NFS client \-\- because inotify reports only what happens on its own host; such a directory is watched from the client's next rescan, every quarter of the idle time\. What it misses is therefore new, and the sweep covers it another way: it counts a file's ctime, which nobody can set back, so a file is kept at least the idle time after it was created or moved in, longer than it takes the client to start watching its directory\.
.P
The \fBserver\fR runs beside the sweep\. The only thing any datagram can make it do is move the access time of an existing regular file inside the table, which keeps it from expiring early: never beyond the maximum age, and nothing is ever created, removed, re\-moded or written\. It runs as its own system user, \fBhdw4s\-shared\-relay\fR, with only \fBCAP_FOWNER\fR, and never sends a packet\. In multicast mode it binds the wildcard \-\- a group arrives addressed to the group \-\- and three checks decide what it reads: only the group it joined reaches the socket; a datagram must be addressed to that group and have arrived on an interface it joined on; and its source must be on a subnet connected to that interface\. \fB\-\-allow\fR (single hosts) narrows the sources further, and \fB\-\-interface\fR the interfaces\. In an unprivileged container systemd's \fBIPAddressAllow=\fR and \fBIPAddressDeny=\fR are silently inert, so the server's own checks are the layer there\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
# /etc/systemd/system/hdw4s\-shared\-relay\-server@srv\-shared\ex2dstore\.service\.d/narrow\.conf
[Service]
Environment=HDW4S_SHARED_RELAY_INTERFACES=br0
Environment=HDW4S_SHARED_RELAY_ALLOW=192\.0\.2\.10
IPAddressAllow=192\.0\.2\.10
IPAddressDeny=any
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The server's user must be able to traverse the store's root (the store rule's 0755): a store root of 0700 makes it drop every announcement, correctly\.
.P
\fBWhen multicast fails, the journal says so\fR, briefly: at start, one line naming the joined interfaces; a client named in \fB\-\-allow\fR that sends no heartbeat for \fB\-\-silence\fR (thirty minutes), one warning, and one line when it is heard again; nothing received from anybody for two heartbeat intervals after somebody was heard, one warning and one recovery line; nothing ever received an hour after start, one line saying to check the firewall and IGMP\. A client that cannot send says so once, with the error, then counts hourly, then says once when it works again\. A join refused because the host has more interfaces than \fBnet\.ipv4\.igmp_max_memberships\fR allows is logged once\. The counters are logged on \fBsystemctl kill \-s USR1\fR, and hourly when they changed; the client's also once when it stops, including every open it did not announce, by reason (\fBdiscard\-\|\.\|\.\|\.\fR, \fBcoalesced\fR), and how many were still pending\. A spoofed source can keep an existing file alive up to its maximum age, and nothing more\.
.P
\fBA burst of opens is paced\.\fR The client sends at most five times a second, packing what is due into full datagrams: at most 100 announcements a second, 1000 at once, and never more than 16 datagrams at once or 20 a second\. The server grows its receive buffer to hold that burst, 278528 bytes as the kernel counts them, and never shrinks a larger one\. Its start line says the size it got, in the same bytes; if that is less, it warns, and \fBnet\.core\.rmem_max\fR must be at least 139264, half of it, because the kernel doubles what it is asked for (a stock rmem_max is enough)\. The datagrams the kernel dropped for the socket \-\- a full buffer, mostly \-\- are \fBdrop\-kernel\-datagrams\fR in its counts line: datagrams, not announcements, of which one can carry dozens\. Opening many thousands of files at once therefore announces them over minutes, at 100 a second\.
.SH "LOGGING"
Counts only\. No file name from the table and no name or payload from the network is ever logged: they are whatever somebody chose, and a journal may be persistent\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
\fB0\fR done, or accepted; \fB1\fR refused by the guard, or \fB\-\-check\fR found a red row; \fB2\fR a usage error, a bad value, or the tool itself failed (never a verdict about the store); \fB3\fR \fB\-\-check\fR found warnings only\.
.SH "CAVEATS"
\fBTrees too deep to reach\.\fR A directory renamed into the bottom of another deep tree can end up deeper than the sweep's depth budget, or deeper than the kernel will resolve a path at all (under AppArmor, about 8 KiB of path)\. Nothing below it is widened or expired; the sweep counts it and \fBhdw4s check\fR warns\. The remedy is the administrator's: on a tmpfs store, reboot, or turn \fB/shared\fR off and on again; on a dataset, move the top ancestor of the deep subtree up by hand (\fBmv\fR within the table), or remove it from a root shell that is not confined by AppArmor\.
.P
The relay client's unit restarts with a growing delay (\fBRestartSteps=\fR, \fBRestartMaxDelaySec=\fR), which needs systemd 254 or later\. An older systemd ignores those two lines with a warning and restarts every \fBRestartSec=\fR instead, which is enough\.
.P
\fBnosymfollow\fR inside a container holds against its users, not against its root: container root can remount without it\. The guard runs at every pass and the expose service unbinds \fB/shared\fR when a flag goes, so the result is an empty \fB/shared\fR, not an unsafe one\. A program that resolves links itself could follow one planted in the table until the watcher removes it\. A file renamed between the sweep's look at it and its removal can be removed while new\. Anything taken from the table is as untrusted as everything else on it; copy it out with a plain \fBcp\fR\.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
hdw4s(8), systemd\.exec(5), fanotify(7), inotify(7), nfs(5)
